    <h1 class="section-title">About OtakuPlay</h1>
    <p class="section-desc">What this is, how the rankings are made, and what happens to your data.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>
      OtakuPlay is a curated, ranked collection of {{ '271' }} titles — 132 anime spanning
      {{ site.firstYear }}–{{ site.lastYear }} and 139 PC games spanning 1992–{{ site.lastYear }}.
      Everything is grouped by decade so you can see how each medium evolved, rather than
      scrolling one flat list where the 1970s never get a look in.
    </p>

    <h2>How rankings are chosen</h2>
    <p>
      These are editorial picks, not an aggregate score scraped from review sites. A title earns
      its place by mattering — for craft, for influence, or because it defined what came after it.
      That means some beloved shows are missing and some divisive ones are here.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tier S</strong> — landmark works. The ones you'd hand someone to explain the medium.</li>
      <li><strong>Tier A</strong> — excellent and worth your time, without quite reshaping the field.</li>
      <li><strong>Rating</strong> — a 0–10 score reflecting overall quality, shown with a star summary.</li>
      <li><strong>Awards</strong> — badges mark Game of the Year, Anime of the Year and critics' picks where they apply.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Disagree with a placement? <a href="{{ site.repo }}/issues/new?title=Pick%20suggestion" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open an issue</a> —
      the whole catalogue lives in a single file in the repo.
    </p>

    <h2>Your library stays yours</h2>
    <p>
      Statuses, personal ratings, notes and favorites are stored in your browser's local storage.
      There is no account, no login and no server: nothing you track is transmitted anywhere, and
      nobody — including us — can see it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The trade-off is that it's per-device and per-browser. Use <strong>Export library</strong> in
      the ⋯ menu to save a JSON copy, and <strong>Import</strong> to restore it elsewhere or after
      clearing your browser data.
    </p>

    <h2>Cover art and data</h2>
    <p>
      Artwork is loaded from the official sources — <a href="https://anilist.co" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AniList</a>
      for anime and the Steam store for games — and remains the property of its respective rights
      holders. Nothing is rehosted here. Five titles have no cover because they aren't sold on
      Steam; those fall back to a gradient.
    </p>
    <p>
      Streaming and store badges show where a title was available at the time of writing and may
      drift as licensing changes.
    </p>

    <h2>Built with</h2>
    <p>
      Plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no runtime framework, assembled by
      <a href="https://www.11ty.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Eleventy</a> and served as a
      static site. It's an installable PWA that works offline after the first visit, and the
      whole thing is <a href="{{ site.repo }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open source</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
